Tonnage Ghast

Ingredients: NEW WAVE + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Risers  
Creative Catalyst: The genre apes the call-and-response of container ships greeting each other in fog.

Tonnage Ghast marries the icy synthesizers and flanged, chorus-drenched basslines of 1980s new wave with the claustrophobic dread of nautical blind navigation. Songs are built not around pop hooks, but around colossal, low-frequency brass glides and rising mechanical tension, evoking two steel leviathans blowing steam-horns past one another in zero-visibility harbor water.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 68–76 BPM. The pulse is a dragging, motorik clatter—halved dance beats driven by cold electronic snares that crack like cargo cranes, underneath which rests a sluggish, hydro-mechanical sway.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Locrian modes bathed in minor-second dissonances. Synthesizers play suspended chords that stretch until they mimic the acoustic beat frequencies of twin motors drifting out of phase.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Tonnage Glissando Risers: Synthesized foghorns slowly pitched up over forty-eight bars via analog pitch ribbons, building unbearable acoustic pressure without ever exploding into an EDM-style drop.
  2. Ballast-Trough Acoustic Delay: An impossible studio method wherein synth outputs are fed through hydrophones submerged in pressurized seawater tanks inside decommissioned cargo holds to capture the physical resonance of rusting bulkheads.
  3. Radar-Gate Panning: Vocals and synth motifs hard-panned from far left to far right in slow, sweeping arcs, mimicking sweeping navigational radar beams slicing through thick weather.
- Dynamics: Long, breathless expanses of damp silence and cavernous bass rumbles suddenly bisected by deafening, multi-octave horn blasts; tension accumulates continuously through endless micro-tonal risers before collapsing abruptly into wet, reverberant stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
Tonnage Ghast crawled out of the North Sea container terminals of Europoort and Bremerhaven during the freezing maritime strike of 1984. Unemployed shipyard apprentices, coldwave musicians, and night-shift crane operators broke into derelict dry docks, running miles of power cords from high-voltage tugboat outlets into analog synths and rewired PA speakers. Their subculture rejected both gothic melodrama and new wave hedonism: followers dressed in oversized foul-weather oilskins smeared with zinc dust, steel-toed boots, and reflective pilot harnesses. At their clandestine dockside rituals, called "Blinds," two separate stages stood kilometers apart on opposite quays, exchanging bellowing horn-synth phrases through dense North Sea advection fog while spectators stood shivering along the breakwaters, tracking the sonic geometry by ear.

Typical Instruments: Roland Juno-60 (chorus-heavy cold pads), fretless bass run through Electro-Harmonix Electric Mistress flangers, Simmons SDS-V drum brain (tuned down to sound like hollow iron holds), Korg MS-20 (processing white-noise steam hiss), and pneumatic air-horn triggers fed into multi-second tape delays.

For Fans Of: The Cure (late-Faith / Pornography era), Coil, Tuxedomoon, This Heat.

